<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  10.10 The Binomial Series and Applications of Taylor Series
-->
<section xml:id="chapter-binomial-series" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>The Binomial Series and Applications of Taylor Series</title>

<objectives>
<introduction>
<p>
After completing this section, you should be able to:
</p>
</introduction>

<ul>
<li>
<p>
Write down the binomial series for <m>(1+x)^m</m> and compute the
binomial coefficients <m>\binom{m}{k}</m> for any real power <m>m</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Explain why the series terminates when <m>m</m> is a non-negative
integer and is infinite otherwise.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Expand functions such as <m>(x+1)^5</m>, <m>(x+1)^{-2}</m>, and
<m>(x+1)^{1/2}</m> as binomial series.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</objectives>

<introduction>
<p>
We have already seen one of the applications of the Taylor series,
namely approximating a function by a polynomial of degree <m>n</m>. In
this section we study two more applications of the Taylor series: the
binomial series and estimating/evaluating non-elementary integrals.
</p>

<p>
Consider the Taylor series generated by <m>f(x) = (1+x)^m</m> at
<m>x = 0</m>, i.e.

<md>
1 + mx + \frac{m(m-1)}{2!}x^2 + \cdots
+ \frac{m(m-1)(m-2)\cdots(m-k+1)}{k!}x^k + \cdots.
</md>
</p>

<p>
This series is known as the <term>binomial series</term>.
</p>
</introduction>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-binomial-series">
<title>Binomial Series</title>

<assemblage xml:id="summary-binomial-series">
<title>Binomial Series</title>
<p>
For <m>-1 \lt x \lt 1</m>,
<md>
(1+x)^m = 1 + \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \binom{m}{k} x^k
</md>
where
<md>
\binom{m}{1} = m, \qquad
\binom{m}{2} = \frac{m(m-1)}{2},
</md>
and
<md>
<mrow>\binom{m}{k} \amp = \frac{m!}{k!(m-k)!}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac{m(m-1)(m-2)\cdots(m-k+2)(m-k+1)}{k!}
\quad\text{for } k \ge 3.</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</assemblage>

<p>
<term>Remark:</term> If <m>m</m> is an integer greater than or equal to
zero, the series stops after <m>(m+1)</m> terms because the
coefficients from <m>k = m+1</m> on are zero, and if <m>m</m> is not a
positive integer or zero, the series is infinite and converges for
<m>|x| \lt 1</m>.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-binomial-positive">
<title>The power is a positive integer</title>
<statement>
<p>
Use binomial series to write the function <m>f(x) = (x+1)^5</m> as a
series.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
We apply the binomial series with <m>m = 5</m>:

<md>
<mrow>(1+x)^5 \amp = 1 + \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \binom{5}{k} x^k</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 1 + \binom{5}{1}x + \binom{5}{2}x^2
+ \binom{5}{3}x^3 + \binom{5}{4}x^4</mrow>
<mrow>\amp \quad + \binom{5}{5}x^5 + \binom{5}{6}x^6
+ \binom{5}{7}x^7 + \cdots</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
Note that for <m>k = 6</m>,
<md>
\binom{5}{6} = \frac{5 \cdot 4 \cdot 3 \cdot 2 \cdot 1 \cdot 0}{6!} = 0,
</md>
in fact for any <m>k \gt 5</m>, it is true that
<m>\binom{5}{k} = 0</m> and therefore

<md>
<mrow>(1+x)^5 \amp = 1 + \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \binom{5}{k} x^k</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 1 + \binom{5}{1}x + \binom{5}{2}x^2 + \binom{5}{3}x^3
+ \binom{5}{4}x^4 + \binom{5}{5}x^5</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 1 + 5x + 10x^2 + 10x^3 + 5x^4 + x^5</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</example>

<example xml:id="example-binomial-negative">
<title>The power is a negative integer</title>
<statement>
<p>
Use binomial series to write the function <m>f(x) = (x+1)^{-2}</m> as a
series.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
We apply the binomial series with <m>m = -2</m>:

<md>
(1+x)^{-2} = 1 + \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \binom{-2}{k} x^k
</md>
</p>

<p>
The first coefficients are

<md>
\binom{-2}{1} = -2, \qquad
\binom{-2}{2} = \frac{(-2)(-2-1)}{2} = 3,
</md>
</p>

<p>
and for general <m>k</m>,

<md>
<mrow>\binom{-2}{k} \amp
= \frac{(-2)(-2-1)(-2-2)(-2-3)\cdots(-2-k+1)}{k!}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = (-1)^k \frac{(k+1)!}{k!} = (-1)^k (k+1).</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
Hence

<md>
<mrow>(1+x)^{-2} \amp = 1 + \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \binom{-2}{k} x^k
= 1 - 2x + 3x^2 + \sum_{k=3}^{\infty} (-1)^k (k+1) x^k</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 1 - 2x + 3x^2 - 4x^3 + 5x^4 - 6x^5 + \cdots
+ (-1)^k (k+1) x^k + \cdots</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
Note that this series converges if <m>-1 \lt x \lt 1</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</example>

<example xml:id="example-binomial-fractional">
<title>The power is not an integer</title>
<statement>
<p>
Use binomial series to write the function
<m>f(x) = (x+1)^{\frac12}</m> as a series. Compute the five non-zero
terms.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
We apply the binomial series with <m>m = \frac12</m>:

<md>
(1+x)^{\frac12} = 1 + \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \binom{\frac12}{k} x^k
</md>
</p>

<p>
The coefficients are

<md>
<mrow>\binom{\frac12}{1} \amp = \frac12,</mrow>
<mrow>\binom{\frac12}{2} \amp
= \frac{\frac12\left(\frac12 - 1\right)}{2}
= \frac{\frac12\left(-\frac12\right)}{2},</mrow>
<mrow>\binom{\frac12}{3} \amp
= \frac{\frac12\left(\frac12 - 1\right)\left(\frac12 - 2\right)}{3!}
= \frac{\frac12\left(-\frac12\right)\left(-\frac32\right)}{3!},</mrow>
<mrow>\binom{\frac12}{4} \amp
= \frac{\frac12\left(\frac12 - 1\right)\left(\frac12 - 2\right)
\left(\frac12 - 3\right)}{4!}
= \frac{\frac12\left(-\frac12\right)\left(-\frac32\right)
\left(-\frac52\right)}{4!}.</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
Hence the five non-zero terms are

<md>
(1+x)^{\frac12}
= 1 + \sum_{k=1}^{\infty} \binom{\frac12}{k} x^k
= 1 + \frac12 x - \frac{x^2}{8} + \frac{x^3}{16}
- \frac{5x^4}{128} + \cdots
</md>
</p>

<p>
<xref ref="fig-binomial-sqrt"/> shows the function
<m>y = \sqrt{1+x}</m> together with the partial sums of this series on
the interval of convergence <m>-1 \lt x \lt 1</m>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-binomial-sqrt">
<caption>
The function <m>y = \sqrt{1+x}</m> and the partial sums of its binomial
series with two, three, and five terms. On the interval
<m>-1 \lt x \lt 1</m> the partial sums approach the function.
</caption>
<image width="78%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-binomial-sqrt">
<diagram dimensions="(400,280)" margins="12">
<definition>f(t) = (t, (1 + t)^(1/2))</definition>
<definition>q1(t) = (t, 1 + t/2)</definition>
<definition>q2(t) = (t, 1 + t/2 - t^2/8)</definition>
<definition>q4(t) = (t, 1 + t/2 - t^2/8 + t^3/16 - 5*t^4/128)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-1.3,-0.35,1.3,1.9)">
<grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<parametric-curve at="bs-q1" function="q1" domain="(-1.0,1.0)"
                  stroke="#E69F00" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="bs-q2" function="q2" domain="(-1.0,1.0)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="bs-q4" function="q4" domain="(-1.0,1.0)"
                  stroke="#009E73" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="bs-f" function="f" domain="(-0.998,1.0)"
                  stroke="black" thickness="3" dash="7 4"/>
<label at="bs-f-lab" anchor="(-1.22,1.78)" alignment="e">
<m>y=\sqrt{1+x}</m>
</label>
<label at="bs-q1-lab" anchor="(-1.22,1.55)" alignment="e" color="#E69F00">
<m>1+\frac{x}{2}</m>
</label>
<label at="bs-q2-lab" anchor="(-1.22,1.32)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2">
<m>1+\frac{x}{2}-\frac{x^2}{8}</m>
</label>
<label at="bs-q4-lab" anchor="(-1.22,1.09)" alignment="e" color="#009E73">
<m>\text{five terms}</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The curve y equals the square root of one plus x, drawn as a dashed black line on the interval from negative one to one, together with three partial sums of its binomial series: the linear approximation in orange, the quadratic in blue, and the five term partial sum in green. All curves pass through zero comma one, and the five term sum is nearly indistinguishable from the function across the interval.">
  <annotation ref="bs-f" text="The function y equals the square root of one plus x, dashed black."/>
  <annotation ref="bs-q1" text="The two term partial sum, one plus x over two."/>
  <annotation ref="bs-q2" text="The three term partial sum, which adds the quadratic correction."/>
  <annotation ref="bs-q4" text="The five term partial sum, which is very close to the function on the whole interval."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</example>
</subsection>

</section>
